WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.701
The payment service sends pay-16 to the partition's leader, the broker that appends it to the log.

2
00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:11.178
The leader writes it at offset 16.

3
00:00:11.178 --> 00:00:13.457
But its answer is lost on the way.

4
00:00:13.457 --> 00:00:17.411
After 30 seconds, the producer only knows it heard nothing.

5
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.599
So it retries.

6
00:00:18.599 --> 00:00:24.949
The leader can't tell a retry from a new record, so it writes pay-16 again, at offset 17.

7
00:00:24.949 --> 00:00:27.945
Every consumer will see the payment twice.

8
00:00:28.133 --> 00:00:33.344
Rewind, and switch on the idempotent producer, the default since Kafka 3.0.

9
00:00:33.344 --> 00:00:40.221
At start-up the producer gets an id from the broker, 5, and numbers its records for each partition.

10
00:00:40.433 --> 00:00:44.000
pay-16 now carries producer 5 and sequence 16.

11
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:49.040
The leader writes it and remembers that producer 5 sends 17 next.

12
00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.056
Again, the answer is lost.

13
00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:54.157
The retry carries the same id and sequence.

14
00:00:54.157 --> 00:01:01.636
The leader finds sequence 16 among the batches it remembers, writes nothing, and answers with offset 16 again.

15
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:02.520
Now order.

16
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.046
The producer keeps up to five requests in flight.

17
00:01:06.046 --> 00:01:10.652
pay-17's request times out, and pay-18 reaches the leader first.

18
00:01:10.833 --> 00:01:15.353
Without idempotence, pay-18 would be written ahead of pay-17.

19
00:01:15.353 --> 00:01:20.169
With idempotence, the leader expects 17, sees 18, and refuses it.

20
00:01:20.567 --> 00:01:27.677
The producer resends pay-17, then pay-18, and both are written once, in the order they were sent.

21
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.493
It only covers the producer's own retries.

22
00:01:30.493 --> 00:01:38.025
If your code catches a timeout and calls send again, that's a new record with a new sequence, and it can be written twice.

23
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:42.690
A producer id and a sequence number let the leader spot a retry.

24
00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:46.311
Then retries can't duplicate or reorder a partition.
